<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sudirman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1916">1916</a>–<a href="/years/1950">1950</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as General Sudirman</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sudirman was the first commander-in-chief of the Indonesian armed forces during the war of independence against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. A former schoolteacher, he was elected commander in November 1945 at the age of 29. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> seized the republican capital Yogyakarta in December 1948 he led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> resistance for months while gravely ill with tuberculosis, carried on a litter. He died in January 1950 and is honoured as a national hero.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sudirman.jpg" alt="Sudirman" width="320" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: IPPHOS - Indonesia Press Photo Service / Indonesian Ministry of Defense · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sudirman.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sudirman was born in Purbalingga in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a> and adopted by his uncle, the sub-district head Raden Cokrosunaryo, who gave him the noble title Raden; he was not told until he was eighteen that Cokrosunaryo was not his birth father. Raised in Cilacap, he studied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> under Kyai Hajji Qahar and was active in Hizboel Wathan, the scouting body of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammadiyah">Muhammadiyah</a>. In 1936 he married Alfiah, daughter of a batik merchant, and began teaching at a Muhammadiyah school, becoming its headmaster without a teacher's certificate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Under the Japanese occupation he joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/defenders-of-the-homeland">Defenders of the Homeland</a> (PETA) in 1944 and commanded a battalion at Kroya. When PETA troops under Kusaeri rebelled on 21 April 1945, he agreed to stop them only if they would not be harmed, and talked them into surrender. He and his men were then interned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogor">Bogor</a>, from which he led a break-out after the proclamation of independence. Back in Banyumas he and Iskaq Tjokrohadisurjo forced the Japanese commander there to hand over his weapons, which made his unit one of the best equipped in the country.</p><p>He took part in the talks that produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linggadjati-agreement">Linggadjati Agreement</a> of 15 November 1946, though he opposed it, and helped form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesian-national-armed-forces">Indonesian National Armed Forces</a> (TNI) on 3 June 1947. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> launched Operation Product on 21 July 1947, Indonesian lines crumbled quickly. After the Dutch attack on Yogyakarta he burned sensitive papers and left with a small group and his doctor, travelling through Kretek, Wonogiri, Ponorogo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kediri-kingdom">Kediri</a>; near Trenggalek republican soldiers, not recognising him, briefly held his party as suspected spies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In August 1949 he asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> to continue the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war, doubting that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> would keep the Roem-Royen Agreement, and threatened to resign when refused, but stayed so as not to unsettle the republic. On 27 December 1949, the day of Dutch recognition, he was reconfirmed as commander-in-chief. He died in Magelang on 29 January 1950, and thousands lined the streets as his convoy passed to Yogyakarta, where he lies in Semaki Heroes' Cemetery. His 100-kilometre guerrilla route is walked by Indonesian cadets, and he was declared a National Hero on 10 December 1964.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sudirman” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Indonesian armed forces, today the Tentara Nasional Indonesia (TNI), were born in the revolution of 1945–1949, when regular units and irregular militias fought the Dutch with guerrilla tactics under commanders such as <b>General Sudirman</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Magelang, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-7.47,110.22&amp;year=1938" aria-label="Magelang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7599 2443 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Sudirman"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8049 2713h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8049 2713h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=-7.47,110.22&amp;year=1938">What was here in 1938? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
